 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "REST AND BE THANKFUL. At the top of the pass out of Glen Croe is a stone seat with those words cut on it, put up by the soldiers who built the military road here under Major William Caulfeild. The modern road still slides off the hill in heavy rain, so the name has never been more useful.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_rest",
   "title": "Rest and Be Thankful"
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE PASS OF BRANDER. In 1308 Robert the Bruce, fighting the MacDougalls of Lorn, held the narrow pass between Ben Cruachan and Loch Awe and had his archers on the slopes above it. Rocks and arrows from above did the rest, as they have in Scotland on more than one occasion.",
   "plaque": "w55_sc_brander",
   "title": "The Pass of Brander"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "CRUACHAN. The 'hollow mountain': a power station was blasted out of the inside of Ben Cruachan and opened in 1965. At night, water is pumped up to a loch high above, and in the day it runs back down through the turbines. The mountain is a giant battery with sheep on it."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 29,
   "text": "KILCHURN CASTLE. A tower house on a point of Loch Awe, begun about 1450 by Sir Colin Campbell of Glenorchy. It was left to fall into ruin in the eighteenth century, and has been the most photographed thing on the loch ever since."
  }
 ],
